# Line endings.
#
# We develop on Windows and deploy to Ubuntu. A shell script or systemd unit
# saved with CRLF is not a cosmetic problem — it is a hard failure on the
# server:
#
#   /opt/glowstar/deploy/install.sh: line 55: syntax error near
#   unexpected token `$'do\r''
#
# That is a real error from a real test run, caused by editing install.sh on
# Windows. systemd units fail the same way (the \r becomes part of the value of
# the last field on every line, so ExecStart points at a binary that does not
# exist). Force LF on everything that Linux executes or parses, regardless of
# what the editor or a contributor's core.autocrlf happens to be set to.

*.sh            text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
*.timer         text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf
*.env           text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf

# Python is run by an interpreter that tolerates either, but keeping it uniform
# stops diffs from showing whole-file changes when someone's editor differs.
*.py            text eol=lf

# Never let Git touch these — a "line ending fix" inside a binary corrupts it.
*.xlsx          binary
*.xls           binary
*.joblib        binary
*.pkl           binary
*.gz            binary
*.zip           binary
